  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1538",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Bunocephalus doriae Boulenger, 1902</li><li>Common name: Banjo Catfish</li><li>Type locality: Villa Rica (Villarrica), Paraguay; Posadas, Río Paraná, Argentina.</li><li>Etymology: Bunocephalus, from the Greek bounos, meaning hill, and kephale, meaning head; in reference to the bumps on the head of the fish, which is particularly marked in some species.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_doriae.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Paraguay-Paraná and Uruguay River basins, of Argentina, Brazil, Paraguay and Uruguay. Type locality: Villa Rica, Paraguay; and Posadas, Río Paraná [Argentina].</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1538",
      "html": "<p>South America: Paraguay-Paraná and Uruguay River basins (La Plata, Paraná, Paraguay, Uruguay, Middle Paraná and Bermejo).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_doriae.htm",
      "html": "<p>Inhabits clearwater streams with sandy to muddy bottom, up to 2.5 m deep, with water flow up to 50 cm/sec. Supposedly an air-breather that hides amidst plant debris on the bottom during the day.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1538",
      "html": "<p>Inhabits clearwater streams with sandy to muddy bottom, up to 2.5 m deep, with water flow up to 50 cm/sec. Supposedly an air-breather that hides amidst plant debris on the bottom during the day.</p><p>IUCN Red List category: Least Concern (last assessed 2020).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_doriae.htm",
      "html": "<p>Very peaceful species which will do well in a quiet community tank where the tank mates are non-aggressive. Does not bury itself in the substrate as much as other species of this family. Provide a sand substrate and a planted dark area to make them feel more secure in their surroundings.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_doriae.htm",
      "html": "<p>Live and frozen foods such as mosquito larvae, tubifex and small crustacea. Tablet foods and sunken flake that lies on the bottom is also taken. In its natural habitat it feeds mainly on chironomid larvae and oligochaetes, besides larvae of several aquatic insects.</p><p>Better to feed at lights out or in a dimly lit tank so they can get their share of any foodstuffs.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1538",
      "html": "<p>In nature, this fish feeds on insect larvae, such as bloodworms.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1538",
      "html": "<p>Unreported. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_doriae.htm",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: The genus name Bunocephalus: buno-, mound or knob; cephalus, head, referring to rounded cusps or bumps on the head. The specific name doriae: in honour of G. Doria, the Italian collector and zoologist.</p><p>IUCN Red List: despite the limited information available on this species, it is assessed as Least Concern due to its wide range and lack of direct threats (IUCN 2021).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_doriae.htm",
      "html": "<p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628.</p><p>Frederico, R.G. 2021. Bunocephalus doriae. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2021.</p><p>Friel, J.P., 2003. Aspredinidae (Banjo catfishes). p. 261-267. In R.E. Reis, S.O. Kullander and C.J. Ferraris, Jr. (eds.) Checklist of the Freshwater Fishes of South and Central America. Porto Alegre: EDIPUCRS, Brasil.</p><p>Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2019. FishBase. World Wide Web electronic publication. www.fishbase.org, ( 08/2019 ).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
